b='content/os/02-threads-scheduling/';p=b+'sched-multicore.md';q=b+'sched-multicore.questions.json'
s=['https://docs.kernel.org/scheduler/sched-bwc.html'];n=['https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/mm/numa_memory_policy.html'];k=['https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/cpu-management-policies/'];g=['https://go.dev/blog/container-aware-gomaxprocs'];py=['https://docs.python.org/3/library/concurrent.futures.html'];f=['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/torvalds/linux/v6.12/kernel/sched/fair.c']
edit(p,'A modern server has dozens or hundreds, arranged in a hierarchy of hyper-threads, cores, shared caches and memory nodes.','Servers may expose many logical CPUs, arranged in hardware-dependent hierarchies of SMT threads, cores, caches and memory nodes.','Not all servers have this count/topology.',f)
edit(p,'Two busy siblings each run noticeably slower than two separate cores.','Resource contention can make two busy siblings slower than using separate physical cores; the effect depends on the workloads.','Performance comparison is not universal.',f)
edit(p,'With one global queue, every scheduling decision on every CPU takes the same lock, which doesn\'t scale past a few cores, and a thread lands on whichever CPU happens to be free, so it hops around and loses its cache.','In a simple global-queue design, scheduling decisions contend for one lock and may move tasks between CPUs. The scaling limit and cache cost depend on implementation and workload; a global queue can also incorporate affinity heuristics.','Remove unsupported core threshold and inevitability.',f)
edit(p,'Load is measured not as a count of tasks but with PELT, a decaying average of each task\'s recent utilisation, weighted by nice.','Balancing considers several signals, including runnable task counts and PELT averages. PELT tracks load, runnable time and running utilization separately; weighted load and CPU utilization are not interchangeable.','Linux uses both task counts and separate load/util signals.',f)
edit(p,'Kubernetes\'s static CPU manager uses this to give pods exclusive cores.','Kubernetes\'s static CPU manager can give eligible Guaranteed containers with integer CPU requests exclusive logical CPUs. Whole-core allocation additionally depends on policy options such as `full-pcpus-only`.','Static policy does not universally allocate exclusive full cores.',k)
edit(p,'Remote accesses typically take something like 1.5 to 2 times as long as local ones, and the interconnect has less bandwidth than local memory.','Remote access cost and interconnect bandwidth depend on the machine and workload.\n\n> [!note] Content gap\n> No reproducible machine-specific NUMA measurements accompany this lesson, so no universal remote/local latency multiplier is provided.','Unsupported benchmark ratio removed visibly.',n)
edit(p,'Linux\'s default memory policy is **first touch**: a page is allocated on the node of the CPU that first writes to it.','Linux normally prefers local allocation. For new private anonymous memory, the first write fault commonly allocates a page near the executing CPU. Explicit policies, cpusets, fallback under memory pressure, huge pages and later migration can change placement.','First-write shorthand is not universal allocation guarantee.',n)
edit(p,'Every page is on node 0.','Assume the main thread stays on node 0, local memory is sufficient, writes fault in new pages and no policy or migration changes placement. The pages then start on node 0.','Supply missing placement assumptions.',n)
edit(p,'no locality, but no hot spot either.','distributed allocation rather than guaranteed per-thread locality. It can reduce concentration on one node, but does not guarantee removal of all hot spots.','Interleaving can still include local pages and workload hot spots.',n)
edit(p,'because a thread moved away from its memory makes every access remote until the memory follows.','because moving a thread can turn accesses to its existing memory into remote accesses; cache hits and pages already elsewhere are exceptions.','Every access remote ignores caches/shared distribution.',n)
edit(p,'The quota is consumed by **all threads on all CPUs combined**. When it runs out, every thread in the group is **throttled** until the next period starts, even if the machine has idle cores.','Fair-class CPU time is charged across the group. Exhausted local bandwidth allocations cause throttling even with idle CPUs. Ancestor quotas, per-CPU runtime accounting and configured burst credit also affect the exact timeline.','Bandwidth cap is fair-class and hierarchical with accounting caveats.',s)
edit(p,'A burst of requests makes all 8 threads busy.','In an idealized model, all 8 run simultaneously from the period boundary, with no other contention, overhead, burst credit or carried local runtime.','Exact 25/75ms outcome requires model assumptions.',s)
edit(p,'A request that needs 5 ms of CPU and arrives at t = 26 ms waits until t = 100 ms before it gets any: about 79 ms of latency for 5 ms of work.','A request arriving at t = 26 ms waits at least 74 ms for replenishment in this model. If it then immediately receives a CPU for 5 ms, its latency is 79 ms; queueing behind existing work can add more.','Original exact latency ignored busy worker queue.',s)
edit(p,'Many organisations do this; the trade-off is weaker isolation from noisy neighbours.','The trade-off is weaker isolation from competing workloads; inherited parent quotas can still throttle the group.','Unsupported adoption generalization and ancestorquota caveat.',s)
edit(p,'**Use exclusive cores** (Kubernetes static CPU manager with integer CPU requests)','**Use exclusive CPUs** (eligible Guaranteed containers under Kubernetes static CPU management; whole cores require appropriate policy options)','Exclusive logicalCPUvsphysicalcore.',k)
edit(p,'Go before 1.25 set `GOMAXPROCS` to the host CPU count; since Go 1.25 it defaults to the cgroup quota when lower.','Go 1.25 introduced container-aware defaults: logical CPUs, affinity and cgroup CPU bandwidth all participate. Fractional quotas are rounded up, and the default generally stays at least 2 unless CPU count or affinity is lower. Explicit settings and compatibility controls can override this.','Default not simply minquota, versionsetting matters.',g)
edit(p,'So a `ProcessPoolExecutor()` with no arguments, inside a 2-CPU container on a 64-core host, starts 64 workers that are throttled together. Read the quota yourself:','For example, 64 visible logical CPUs, no narrower affinity and no Python CPU-count override can yield a default maximum of 64 processes despite a two-CPU quota. Workers start as jobs are submitted, with behavior depending on Python version and start method. A simple cgroup-v2 quota reader is:', 'Constructor does not eagerlystartallworkers; coreslogicaldistinction.',py)
edit(p,'# "200000 100000" -> 2.0','# "200000 100000" -> 2.0\n# Assumes this path is the relevant cgroup.', 'Path notuniversallyowncgroup.',s)
edit(p,'## Pitfalls','This helper reads one visible cgroup only. It does not discover the process\'s cgroup or ancestor limits, handle cgroup v1, or combine quota with affinity. An unlimited leaf can still have a constrained ancestor.\n\n## Pitfalls','Clarify helper limitations instead of claiming robust quota discovery.',s)
qe(q,'sched-multicore-q3','prompt','A fair-class batch container has cpu.weight=10, no leaf or ancestor quota, access to all 16 physical cores, and enough runnable workers. With no competing work, how much CPU can it use?','Need parallelworkers and unrestrictedancestor/affinity.',s)
qe(q,'sched-multicore-q4','prompt','In an idealized quota model, a container has cpu.max = 300000 100000. Ten threads run continuously on ten CPUs from each period boundary, without contention, overhead, burst credit or carried runtime. How long is the group throttled in each 100 ms period?','Explicit model assumptions preserve arithmetic without claiming exactmeasurement.',s)
qe(q,'sched-multicore-q5','prompt','A main thread pinned to NUMA node 0 writes every page of a new private 100 GiB array, with enough local memory and no explicit policy or migration. Workers then run on both nodes and bandwidth falls below expectations. Which placement problem fits this setup?','No guarantee unpinned thread/allocationfallbackplacesallpagesnode0.',n)
qe(q,'sched-multicore-q5','workedExample','Under the stated assumptions, first-write faults allocate the new pages on node 0. Workers on node 1 then need remote memory for cache misses to those pages, and requests concentrate on node 0. The exact cost is hardware-dependent; no measured multiplier is available here. Parallel initialization, deliberate interleaving or matching workers to memory can improve placement, subject to measurement.','Remove unsupported NUMA ratio and everyaccessclaim.',n)
qe(q,'sched-multicore-q6','prompt','On Linux Python 3.13+, with 64 visible logical CPUs, no CPU-count override or affinity restriction, a pod has a two-CPU quota. A default ProcessPoolExecutor is given enough CPU-bound jobs to fill it. What problem can occur?','VersionlogicalCPUandlazy startup assumptions.',py)
qe(q,'sched-multicore-q6','text','Up to 64 workers compete for two CPUs of aggregate quota, risking heavy throttling','Maximum count notconstructoreagerness.',py,option=1)
qe(q,'sched-multicore-q6','explanation','Correct. The default maximum uses process_cpu_count(), which considers affinity but not CPU bandwidth quota. Running many CPU-bound workers can exhaust each period budget early.','Defaultmaxnotalwaysworkersactual.',py,option=1)
qe(q,'sched-multicore-q6','workedExample','With the specified environment, process_cpu_count() returns 64, so the default maximum is 64 workers. Their CPU time shares the two-CPU quota. Enough runnable workers may exhaust the budget early and then wait for replenishment. Benchmark a smaller pool; discover effective affinity and cgroup hierarchy limits rather than assuming the host count is usable bandwidth.','Remove guaranteed identicalthroughput and insufficientleafpathfix.',py+s)
qe(q,'sched-multicore-q7','explanation','Correct, if no ancestor quota remains: removing the limiting bandwidth cap can remove this cause of throttling. Fairness then depends on competing weights and available CPUs.','Ancestorcapsremain.',s,option=1)
qe(q,'sched-multicore-q7','workedExample','A smaller CPU-bound pool can consume budget more smoothly; removing all relevant quota caps avoids quota throttling; accumulated burst credit can absorb short spikes. Raising weight does not raise the quota. More workers can exhaust it earlier. These are workload-dependent remedies: compare throughput and latency, and inspect ancestor limits.','Avoidabsolute unchangedthrottling and guaranteedimprovement.',s)
qe(q,'sched-multicore-q8','text','Throttling was reported for 40% of the counted periods; request latency needs separate measurement','Counters cannotinfermanyrequestsprobablywait.',s,option=1)
qe(q,'sched-multicore-q8','workedExample','14400 / 36000 = 40%. With continuously active 100 ms enforcement periods, 36000 periods correspond to one hour. These counters alone do not measure request latency or the fraction of wall time stalled. Compare interval deltas, throttled_usec, usage and latency measurements; throttled time can aggregate across per-CPU run queues.','Do notconfuseperiodcountwithwalltime/requestcounts.',s)
qe(q,'sched-multicore-q9','prompt','In an idealized quota model, cpu.max = 50000 100000. One request needs 80 ms CPU and starts at a period boundary. Assume no other work, contention, overhead, burst credit or carried runtime. Roughly when does it finish?','Exact timing simplifiedmodel.',s)
qe(q,'sched-multicore-q11','explanation','Linux considers runnable counts alongside PELT load, runnable and utilization averages, CPU capacity and other signals. It does not rely purely on task counts.','Original falselysaysno countsand conflatesweightedutil.',f,option=4)
qe(q,'sched-multicore-q11','workedExample','The correct statements are 1, 3 and 4. Newidle balancing may pull work; wake-up placement considers locality and idle CPUs; recently running tasks may be treated as cache-hot. Cross-node balancing has different costs. Linux uses multiple signals, including task counts and distinct PELT averages; no one metric describes every balancing decision.','Removeabsolute cachehotnevermove and exclusivelyPELT.',f)
